# NFC hardware contract

Enforced statically by `scripts/test-nfc-hardware.mjs` (50 checks, runs on
Linux CI — no Xcode needed). Run it after touching any file this rule globs.

## NTAG216-only

- The product band is **NXP NTAG216**, HF NFC, **13.56 MHz**, **ISO 14443A
  Type 2**, NDEF blank unlocked (`AppConfig.BraceletRF`). Never source or
  special-case NTAG213, NTAG215, MIFARE, LF (~125 kHz), or UHF (~860–960 MHz).
- Every user-facing capacity/format error must name **NXP NTAG216** by name
  (`NFCWriter.swift`, `ProfileNFCCodec.swift`) so a bad chip is diagnosable
  from the message alone.

## Tap geometry

- Deliberate antenna tap is **~1–2″** (`intentionalTapInchesMin/Max`).
  Walk-by/casual standoff (~6–8″) must never fire a session — RedMed only
  starts CoreNFC on an explicit Write/Scan tap
  (`BraceletRF.requiresExplicitUserSession = true`), never because a band is
  merely nearby.
- Beyond ~4″ (`reliableCouplingInchesMax`) is outside reliable ISO 14443
  coupling — don't advertise longer range in copy.
- Face art is logo-print RedMed heart + wordmark on black silicone
  (`#232425`) at **30×9 mm**. Keep `AppConfig.swift` and
  `owner/RedMed/Document/Document.html` in lockstep on this string.

## Write gate

- `NFCBandManager.writeBand` must refuse: scanner sessions
  (`isScannerSession`), a busy session (`isBusy`), and an empty profile
  (`profile.hasSensitiveProfileData`) — in that order, before touching
  CoreNFC.
- Enforce the **850-byte** NTAG216 cap on the packed `#d=` URL before
  writing; refuse and tell the owner to shorten RedMed rather than
  truncating silently.
- `NFCWriter.writeURL` must refuse anything that is not a
  `AppConfig.OwnerBandURI.isValidWriteURL` (`medicalCardBaseURL#d=…`) —
  never a vendor cloud, social/short link, or App Store URL.
- All hardware entry points stay gated on `AppConfig.nfcHardwareEnabled`
  (single kill switch) and `NFCNDEFReaderSession.readingAvailable`. When
  hardware is parked (`false`), `NFCBandManager` must only pack a URL
  (Copy Band Link / Preview) — it must never open a live CoreNFC session or
  mark the band Linked.

## NDEF contract

- Hand-build the NDEF record as **NFC Forum Well Known Type "U"**
  (`NFCURICodec.wellKnownURIRecord`) so the `#d=` fragment survives iOS
  Background Tag Reading — don't rely on Apple's
  `wellKnownTypeURIPayload(url:)` helper alone; it can drop the fragment.
- The written message is always a single-record `NFCNDEFMessage`. Check
  `message.length` against the tag's reported `capacity` before writing,
  and refuse (don't clip) when it's too big.
- Handle `queryNDEFStatus` exhaustively: `.notSupported` (wrong chip),
  `.readOnly` (locked tag), `.readWrite` (write path), and `@unknown
  default`. Never fall through silently.
- Decoding a chip must go through `NFCURICodec.string(from:)` and
  `ProfileNFCCodec.decodeProfile`, which requires a `#d=` fragment — a bare
  string or URL without it must not decode as a chip profile.

## Read-back requirement

- Every write is followed by a `tag.readNDEF` read-back before reporting
  success. Compare the read-back URL to what was written with
  `NFCURICodec.match` — do not trust `writeNDEF`'s completion alone.
- `success` / `verified` only become `true` when that read-back matches.
  `NFCBandManager.linkBracelet` (marking the bracelet Linked/paired) must
  require `writeVerified`, never just `writeSucceeded`.

## No permanent lock bytes

- The band ships **factory blank and unlocked**
  (`factoryLock = false`, `factoryPreEncode = false`, `isRewritable = true`).
  RedMed must never lock, permalock, or otherwise make the tag read-only.
- A tag that reports `.readOnly` is refused with a "locked/read-only —
  needs NDEF blank unlocked" message, never force-written.

## CoreNFC session rules

- Use only `NFCNDEFReaderSession` (`NFCWriter`, `NFCReader`), never
  `NFCTagReaderSession` — the raw tag session that would allow lock-byte /
  low-level manipulation. NDEF stays the only supported technology.
- Writer sessions use `invalidateAfterFirstRead: false` (write, then
  read-back, then invalidate on this same session). Reader sessions use
  `invalidateAfterFirstRead: true`.
- No Simulator fake-success path anywhere in `NFCWriter` / `NFCReader` —
  failures stay failures; a session only succeeds after a real device
  round-trip.
- Keep `AppConfig.nfcHardwareEnabled`, `RedMed.entitlements`
  (`com.apple.developer.nfc.readersession`), and `Info.plist`'s
  `NFCReaderUsageDescription` in lockstep — restoring hardware means
  flipping all three together (see `docs/NFC-RESTORE.md`), never just the
  Swift flag.
